  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 215 PM CDT Wed Jul 22 2026\n\nRegional surface observations and GOES visible satellite \nimagery depict atmospheric conditions this afternoon that are \nmore typical for September or early October rather than July \nwith fairly expansive, broken stratocumulus coverage noted \nacross the Upper Midwest amidst surface temperatures in the mid\n60s to mid 70s. The intrusion of this early fall-like air mass \nyielded brisk northerly winds over Lake Michigan that helped \nwhip up some choppy waves across the lake's southern shores.\nThese high waves -- along with an associated elevated risk for \nrip currents -- persist at southern Lake Michigan beaches at \nthis hour, but should continue to gradually subside into tonight\nas the center of a ~1022 mb surface high slides closer to the \narea.\n\nDry weather with comfortable humidity levels will continue into\ntomorrow as the surface high positions itself over the southern\nGreat Lakes. Tomorrow's high temperatures will be a few degrees\nhigher than today's highs, and temperatures will warm further \non Friday and into the weekend as the present air mass is \nprodded eastward by a building mid- to upper-level ridge across \nthe central third of the country. An upper-level shortwave will \nalso drift southeastward across the Lower Midwest on Friday and \nwill be accompanied by rain. According to the majority of the \nlatest forecast guidance, the lingering influence of the \naforementioned high pressure should tend to keep this rain \nsuppressed off to our southwest and south. Have thus maintained \na dry forecast for Friday, but couldn't rule out needing to \nintroduce mentionable PoP chances for our southern counties at \nsome point if guidance as a whole trends a little farther to the\nnorth/northeast with this precipitation.\n\nBy early next week, decidedly more July-like conditions will be\nin place across the region once again with temperatures \nbuilding into the upper 80s and 90s. Dew points are also \nexpected to climb into the 70s (and possibly well into the 70s) \nas rich Gulf moisture drifts into the region and is enhanced by \nevapotranspirational processes occurring across cropland. Heat \nindices will thus at least approach, and could potentially \nexceed, the 100 degree mark on Sunday and again on Monday. In \naddition, this juicy air mass will likely support episodic \nthunderstorm chances across the region while it is in place. The\nelevated levels of moisture and instability that will be \npresent across the region during this time frame will likely \nsupport threats for severe thunderstorms and flash flooding \nsomewhere in the Midwest as well, though only time will tell \nprecisely where and when such threats may materialize early next\nweek.\n\nOgorek",
